textosLlegan("theory", {"hf-cinco-vias":{"html":"<p>Rome, around 1266. In the Dominican convent of Santa Sabina, a friar of about forty, heavily built and quiet (according to tradition, his fellow students called him ‘the dumb ox’) and famous for his memory, begins to dictate a new work. His name is Thomas, and he was born in Roccasecca, near Aquino, halfway between Rome and Naples. He has taught in Paris and has already written thousands of pages. In the prologue he explains who the book is for: <strong>beginners</strong>. He wants to set out theology in an order that keeps them from getting lost in pointless questions and repetitions. The work, which he will leave unfinished, is called the <em>Summa Theologiae</em>.</p>\n<p>In question 2 of the first part, Thomas asks whether God exists. The answer takes up a single article, of little more than a page. In it he puts forward <strong>five ways</strong>, five paths leading from what we see in the world to a first principle. That page has been read, commented on and attacked for more than seven hundred years. Yet almost nothing in it was new. Each way takes up an argument with centuries of history behind it: Greek, Christian, Muslim and Jewish. This exploration follows that history backwards and forwards.</p>\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/cinco_vias/gozzoli_triunfo_santo_tomas.jpg\" width=\"570\" height=\"1293\" alt=\"Gilded painting: a Dominican friar seated with an open book, between two bearded sages, and a man in a turban lying at his feet\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Benozzo Gozzoli, <em>Triumph of Saint Thomas Aquinas</em> (around 1470-1475, Louvre Museum). Thomas, in the centre, between Aristotle (on the left) and Plato (on the right); at his feet, Averroes, defeated. The painting expresses how Thomas was seen in his own time: above his sources, although he has them on either side</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: why would someone who already believes in God bother to prove that he exists? Who do you think Thomas is talking to?</p>\n\n<h2>1. Before the proofs: does God’s existence need to be proved?</h2>\n<p>Thomas does not begin with the ways. First he asks two questions. The first is whether God’s existence is <strong>self-evident</strong>, in the way that it is self-evident that the whole is greater than the part. His answer has two sides. In itself, yes: in God, what he is and the fact of existing are the same thing. But <strong>to us, no</strong>, because we do not know what God is. That is why it has to be proved (<em>Summa Theologiae</em>, I, q. 2, a. 1).</p>\n<p>The second question is whether it can be proved, and how. Thomas distinguishes two kinds of proof. One goes from cause to effect: if I know it has rained, I know the ground will be wet. The other goes from effect to cause: if I see the ground wet, I look for what has made it wet. Since we do not know God’s essence, only the second is possible: to start from his effects, which are the things of the world, and rise up to him. That is why the five ways are <strong><em>a posteriori</em></strong> proofs: they start from experience (q. 2, a. 2).</p>\n<p>The most surprising thing comes next. The article of the five ways begins with two objections <strong>against</strong> the existence of God, and they are very up to date:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Objection</th><th>What it says</th><th>What it would be called today</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st</td><td>If an infinite good existed, there would be no evil at all. But there is evil in the world. Therefore God does not exist.</td><td>The problem of evil</td></tr><tr><td>2nd</td><td>What is explained by fewer principles does not need more. Nature explains what is natural, and human reason and will explain what we do on purpose. Therefore there is no need to suppose God.</td><td>The principle of economy (what will later be called ‘Ockham’s razor’) and naturalism</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Thomas writes them himself, in their strongest form, and answers them at the end of the article. That is how scholasticism worked: first the opponent’s reasons are given, then one’s own, and finally each objection is answered.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: why do you think Thomas begins by agreeing with the atheist? Does it seem to you a good method for discussing any subject? Have you ever seen it in a debate today?</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘in the Middle Ages nobody doubted that God existed, so the ways were not addressed to anyone’. Thomas himself sets out the atheist’s objections. And in another work, the <em>Summa contra Gentiles</em>, he explains that with Muslims and pagans one cannot start from any shared Scripture, and so one must argue by reason alone; with Jews, he adds, one can start from the Old Testament (I, 2).</p>\n\n<h2>2. The first forerunner: Plato, Aristotle and the first mover</h2>\n<p>The first way, the way from motion, is the oldest. The first proof ‘from motion’ whose text has survived is by <strong>Plato</strong>, in Book X of the <em>Laws</em> (893b-896b). Plato distinguishes what moves only if something else moves it and what moves itself. The second is the <strong>soul</strong>, and it must be the origin of all motion: if everything needed something else to move it, nothing would ever start moving.</p>\n<p><strong>Aristotle</strong>, Plato’s pupil, gave it the form that Thomas would use. In the <em>Physics</em> (Book VIII) and in the <em>Metaphysics</em> (Book XII, chapters 6 and 7) he reasons like this:</p>\n<ul><li>everything that moves is moved by something else;</li><li>the chain of movers cannot be infinite;</li><li>therefore there is an <strong>unmoved mover</strong>, which moves without being moved.</li></ul>\n<p>That mover has to be <strong>pure actuality</strong>: if it had any potentiality, it could change, and then it would in turn need a mover. And how can something that does not move move anything? Aristotle answers with an image: it moves <strong>as what is loved</strong> moves the one who loves it (<em>Metaphysics</em> XII 7, 1072b3). It does not push: it attracts, as an end.</p>\n<p>But Aristotle’s God is not Thomas’s. For Aristotle, the world is <strong>eternal</strong>: nobody created it. The unmoved mover does not know people or care for them. It thinks only of itself: it is ‘thought thinking itself’ (XII 9, 1074b34). Thomas takes the whole argument, but puts it at the service of a <strong>creator</strong> God who knows and cares for every creature. How the <em>Physics</em> and the <em>Metaphysics</em> reached Paris is told in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-aristoteles-arabe\">‘Aristotle in Arabic’</a>, and Aristotle’s physics in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-aa\">‘Aristotle: the complete system’</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/cinco_vias/aristoteles_busto_altemps.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"1071\" alt=\"Marble bust of a man with a short beard and a high forehead\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bust of Aristotle: Roman marble copy of a Greek bronze by Lysippos (National Roman Museum, Palazzo Altemps)</figcaption></figure></div>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Aristotle’s first mover</th><th>Thomas’s God</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Does it create the world?</td><td>No: the world is eternal</td><td>Yes, out of nothing</td></tr><tr><td>How does it move?</td><td>As an end, by attracting (this is the most common reading)</td><td>As efficient cause and as end</td></tr><tr><td>Does it know the world?</td><td>It thinks only of itself</td><td>It knows each thing, because it has made it</td></tr><tr><td>Is there only one?</td><td>Disputed: in <em>Metaphysics</em> XII 8 he calculates 47 or 55 movers for the celestial spheres</td><td>Only one</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: can you love someone who does not know you exist? And be moved by something that will never answer you? Think of an example from your own life.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘the first mover is the first domino: the one that started everything a long time ago’. For Aristotle the world had no beginning. The first mover is first because <strong>everything depends on it now</strong>, not because it came earlier in time.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Augustine, Boethius and Anselm: climbing the degrees of goodness</h2>\n<p>The Christian tradition before Thomas looked for God along another path: that of <strong>degrees</strong>. If there are things that are more or less good, more or less true, there must be something good and true to the highest degree, which serves as the measure of everything else. The idea comes from Plato: things ‘participate’ in the Ideas, and the Idea of the Good is at the top.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong>, in <em>On Free Choice of the Will</em> (Book II, between 388 and 395), starts from something we all have within us: truth. Our mind does not invent the truths of mathematics: it discovers them, and they are the same for everyone. Therefore there is something higher than the human mind, and that is God or leads to God. His way of thinking is explained in <a href=\"#teoria/hf-platon-agustin\">‘Plato, Saint Augustine and dualism’</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Boethius</strong>, in <em>The Consolation of Philosophy</em> (around 524), written in prison before he was executed, reasons like this: if there are imperfect goods, there must be a perfect good, of which the imperfect ones are a diminished form (Book III, prose 10).</li>\n<li><strong>Anselm of Canterbury</strong>, then prior of the monastery of Bec, in Normandy (he would become abbot in 1078 and Archbishop of Canterbury in 1093), turns it into a proof in the <em>Monologion</em> (1076, chapters 1-4). Things are more or less good, and therefore they are so through something that is good in itself and supremely good. This is the direct root of Thomas’s <strong>fourth way</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>But Anselm was not satisfied. He wanted, as he tells us, a <strong>single</strong> argument, one that would need no other. He found it a year later in the <em>Proslogion</em> (1077-1078, chapter 2), and it is one of the most debated arguments in the history of philosophy: the <strong>ontological argument</strong>. It can be summed up in five steps:</p>\n<ol><li>God is ‘that than which nothing greater can be thought’.</li><li>Even someone who denies God understands that phrase. So it exists at least in their thought.</li><li>Existing in thought and in reality is more than existing in thought alone.</li><li>If that than which nothing greater can be thought existed only in thought, we could think of something greater: the same thing, but existing for real as well. And that is contradictory.</li><li>Therefore it also exists in reality.</li></ol>\n<p>It is an <strong><em>a priori</em></strong> proof: it does not need to look at the world. The concept of God is enough. A monk of Marmoutier, <strong>Gaunilo</strong>, replied straight away with <em>On Behalf of the Fool</em>: by that route, a lost island, the most perfect one that can be thought, would also exist, just because we can think of it. Anselm replied that the argument works only for that than which nothing greater can be thought, because only that cannot be thought not to exist; an island, however perfect, can be thought not to exist. The two texts were published together: Anselm asked for Gaunilo’s criticism always to accompany his book.</p>\n<p><strong>What Thomas did with Anselm</strong> is one of the keys to this exploration. Thomas <strong>accepted</strong> the path of degrees from the <em>Monologion</em>, which becomes his fourth way. But he <strong>rejected</strong> the argument of the <em>Proslogion</em> (<em>Summa Theologiae</em>, I, q. 2, a. 1, ad 2). His answer has two parts:</p>\n<ul><li>Not everyone understands by ‘God’ what Anselm says: some have thought that God was a body.</li><li>And even if it were understood that way, it does not follow from the fact that something is in thought that it exists in reality. It would follow only if one already admitted that something than which nothing greater can be thought exists in reality, which is exactly what the atheist denies.</li></ul>\n<p>The ontological argument did not die with that criticism. Descartes returned to it, in the fifth <em>Meditation</em> (1641; see <a href=\"#teoria/hf-descartes-makro\">‘Descartes and rationalism’</a>), and so did Leibniz. Kant criticised it with a famous phrase: ‘being is not a real predicate’ (section 8).</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about (logic): write out Anselm’s five steps as premises and conclusion. Do you think the conclusion follows from the premises? If so, the argument is valid and, if it fails, it must fail in one of the premises. Which one do Gaunilo and Thomas not accept?</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘the ontological argument is one of the five ways’ or ‘the first way is Anselm’s’. Thomas expressly rejects it. That is why <strong>all</strong> his ways start from experience: motion, causes, what comes into being and passes away, degrees and the order of the world.</p>\n\n<h2>4. The philosophers of Islam and Judaism: the possible and the necessary</h2>\n<p>Between Aristotle and Thomas there is a decisive link: the philosophers and theologians who wrote in Arabic. For centuries they discussed how it can be proved that there is a God, and Thomas read them in Latin: Avicenna, in the Toledo translations (see <a href=\"#teoria/hf-toledo-traductores\">‘Toledo, the city of translations’</a>); Maimonides, in a Latin translation made around 1240 from the Hebrew.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>The <em>kalam</em></strong>, the rational theology of Islam, put forward a very simple argument: the world began to exist, everything that begins to exist has a cause, and therefore the world has a cause. It was defended by theologians such as <strong>al-Ghazali</strong> (11th century) and also by a philosopher, <strong>al-Kindi</strong> (9th century). In Judaism it was used by <strong>Saadia Gaon</strong> (10th century). Everything depends on the first premise: that the world had a beginning.</li>\n<li><strong>Avicenna</strong> (Ibn Sina, 980-1037), in the <em>Metaphysics</em> of his great work <em>The Healing</em> (Book I, chapters 6 and 7, and Book VIII, chapters 1 to 3), changed the question. He distinguished what is <strong>possible in itself</strong> from what is <strong>necessary in itself</strong>. Something is possible in itself when nothing in what it is forces it to exist. It can exist and it can not exist. If it exists, it is because something else makes it exist. But a chain of things that are only possible cannot hold itself up, even if it is infinite: there must be something that exists <strong>in itself</strong>, the necessary in itself. This is the root of the <strong>third way</strong> and of an idea that Thomas makes his own: in creatures, what something is (its essence) and the fact that it exists are different; in God, they are not.</li>\n<li><strong>Averroes</strong> (Ibn Rushd, 1126-1198), from Córdoba, criticised Avicenna and went back to Aristotle’s physical proof, the proof from motion, because he thought it more solid (see <a href=\"#teoria/hf-verdad-no-contradice\">‘Truth does not contradict truth’</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Maimonides</strong> (1138-1204), also from Córdoba, devoted the beginning of Book II of the <em>Guide for the Perplexed</em> (around 1190) to this question. He sets out four proofs that there is a God. One is the proof from the first mover. Another, the third, is very like Thomas’s third way: not everything can come into being and pass away, because then at some moment there would have been nothing. But the most important thing is his strategy: to prove God <strong>even if one grants that the world is eternal</strong>, so that the proof does not depend on something reason cannot establish. Thomas will do exactly the same (see <a href=\"#teoria/hf-judios-andalus\">‘Thinking in Arabic, praying in Hebrew’</a>).</li></ul>\n<p>Thomas does not hide these debts. In his works he quotes Avicenna hundreds of times, and he respectfully calls Maimonides ‘Rabbi Moses’.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: Thomas learnt a great deal about how to prove God’s existence by reading, among others, a Muslim and a Jew. What does that tell you about how ideas travelled in the Middle Ages? Does something similar happen today?</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘the five ways are a Christian invention’. Almost every step has a Greek, Muslim or Jewish antecedent, and Thomas names them in other works. His originality lies in the synthesis: in having brought them together, put them in order and joined them to his own way of understanding being and existence.</p>\n\n<h2>5. The five ways, one by one</h2>\n<p>The full text, in our own translation, is in the commented texts (text 6). Here they are summarised, each with a present-day example.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Way</th><th>Starts from</th><th>Principle</th><th>Why the chain cannot be infinite</th><th>Arrives at</th><th>Main antecedent</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1st, from motion</td><td>There are things that move, that change</td><td>Whatever moves is moved by something else: nothing passes from potentiality to actuality on its own</td><td>The intermediate movers move only if the first moves them, just as the stick moves only if the hand moves it</td><td>A first mover that is moved by nothing</td><td>Aristotle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2nd, from efficient cause</td><td>There is an order of efficient causes</td><td>Nothing is the cause of itself, because it would have to exist before it existed</td><td>If the first cause is taken away, the intermediate ones and the effect disappear</td><td>A first efficient cause</td><td>Aristotle, Avicenna</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3rd, from the possible and the necessary</td><td>There are things that come into being and pass away</td><td>What can not exist, at some time does not exist; and nothing comes from nothing</td><td>If everything could not exist, there would be nothing now; and necessary beings that owe their necessity to another cannot go on to infinity</td><td>Something necessary in itself</td><td>Avicenna, Maimonides</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4th, from degrees</td><td>There are things more and less good, true and noble</td><td>‘More’ and ‘less’ are said according to closeness to a maximum</td><td>It does not use the chain: it rises to the maximum</td><td>Something that is being in the highest degree, cause of all perfection</td><td>Plato, Augustine, Anselm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5th, from the governance of the world</td><td>Things without knowledge act for an end</td><td>What has no knowledge tends to an end only if it is directed by someone who knows</td><td>It does not use the chain: it goes from direction to an intelligence</td><td>An intelligence that orders nature</td><td>Stoics, John of Damascus</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Examples from today</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>1st way:</strong> the water in the saucepan heats up because the flame heats it, the flame because the gas burns, the gas because it comes through the pipe pushed by pressure… Thomas asks what holds up the whole chain of changes <strong>now</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>2nd way:</strong> you exist because oxygen, food, the Earth’s temperature, the Sun’s energy keep you alive… None of these gives itself existence.</li>\n<li><strong>3rd way:</strong> everything you know might never have existed: you, your house, the mountains, the stars. Can the whole world be made only of things that might not exist?</li>\n<li><strong>4th way:</strong> we say that one action is ‘fairer’ than another. Fairer compared with what? It seems that we are comparing with a measure, even though we do not have it in front of us.</li>\n<li><strong>5th way:</strong> a seed always turns into a plant of its own species, and the Earth always goes round the Sun with the same regularity. Is it chance or is there a direction?</li></ul>\n<p>Each way ends with a similar phrase: ‘and this everyone calls God’, ‘to which everyone gives the name of God’, ‘and this we call God’. Thomas does not say that he has already proved everything that faith says about God. He says that he has reached something to which that name is given: the first three ways say ‘everyone’, and the last two, ‘we’.</p>\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/cinco_vias/zurbaran_apoteosis_santo_tomas.jpg\" width=\"900\" height=\"1138\" alt=\"Large Baroque painting: a friar with a quill in his hand rises among clouds, surrounded by bishops and saints; below, an emperor and some friars kneeling\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Francisco de Zurbarán, <em>Apotheosis of Saint Thomas Aquinas</em> (1631, Museum of Fine Arts of Seville). Thomas, with the quill, among the four great teachers of the Latin Church (Ambrose, Gregory, Jerome and Augustine); below, kneeling, the Emperor Charles V and Dominican friars. Painted for the College of Saint Thomas in Seville, it shows the place Thomas had come to occupy three and a half centuries after his death</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: choose the way you find most convincing and the one you find least convincing. At which particular step do you find it hard to follow the reasoning?</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘the five ways are five identical proofs with different examples’. The first three have the same structure: a chain of dependences that cannot be infinite. The fourth and fifth do not talk about chains: one rises to a maximum and the other goes from order to an intelligence.</p>\n<h3>How to read them properly: five clarifications</h3>\n<p><strong>They are a summary.</strong> Thomas writes the <em>Summa</em> for beginners in theology who already knew philosophy, and he takes many things for granted. Each way is the outline of an argument that he develops in other works: the one from motion, at length, in the <em>Summa contra Gentiles</em> (I, 13); the one from the possible and the necessary, also there (I, 15). In <em>On Being and Essence</em> (c. 1255, ch. 4) he starts from another idea: in things, what they are and the fact that they exist are distinct, and this requires a being whose essence is to exist. Some call it a ‘sixth way’. Many misunderstandings come from reading the five ways on their own, as if they were all that Thomas had to say.</p>\n<p><strong>‘Motion’ means change.</strong> The Latin word <em>motus</em>, in Aristotle’s vocabulary, is not only moving from one place to another: it is any passage from potentiality to actuality. Getting warm, growing, learning or ageing are motions in this sense. The first way is not about bodies pushing each other, but about everything that changes.</p>\n<p><strong>The third way has a disputed step.</strong> It says that, if all things could not exist, at some point nothing would have existed. Many commentators see a leap here: from the fact that each thing ceases to exist at some time it does not follow that all things cease to exist at the same time. It would be a <strong>fallacy of composition</strong>, like thinking that, if each brick weighs a kilo, the whole wall weighs a kilo. There are two ways of saving the argument. One assumes an infinite time, in which every possibility ends up being fulfilled, including the possibility that nothing is left. The other prefers the version in the <em>Summa contra Gentiles</em> (I, 15), which does not use that step: what can be and not be, if it exists, has a cause that makes it exist.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about (logic): each member of a family has a mother. Does the whole family have a mother? Find another example in which what holds for each part does not hold for the whole, and another in which it does.</p>\n<p><strong>The fourth way does not work for just any scale.</strong> Thomas is not talking about any quality that admits of degrees, but about perfections that carry no limit with them: being, goodness, truth, nobility. These are called the <strong>transcendentals</strong>, because everything that exists has them simply by existing. Being red or being large, on the other hand, presuppose a body, and a body is limited. The example of fire, ‘the hottest of all’, is only a comparison taken from Aristotle.</p>\n<p><strong>Trap case:</strong> ‘by that rule, since some people smell worse than others, there would have to be someone who smells worse than anyone’. This is Richard Dawkins’s objection in <em>The God Delusion</em> (2006). The fourth way does not apply to just any comparison, only to perfections without limit. Whether that restriction saves the argument is still being debated.</p>\n<p><strong>The fifth way: why an intelligence is needed.</strong> Thomas is thinking of Aristotle’s <strong>final cause</strong>: that towards which something tends. When the seed begins to grow, the plant does not yet exist. And what does not exist cannot direct anything, unless it exists beforehand in a mind, as the statue exists in the sculptor’s head before he carves it. That is why he concludes that the order of things that lack knowledge lies in an intelligence. The starting point is not living beings, but anything that always acts in the same way: today an electron, which is always attracted by a proton, would serve as an example.</p>\n\n<h2>6. What the ways do not say</h2>\n<h3>They do not say that the world had a beginning</h3>\n<p>This is the most common misunderstanding. Thomas held, against many theologians of his time, such as Bonaventure, that <strong>reason cannot prove that the world had a beginning</strong>. He believed that it did, because the Bible says so, but he maintained that this is known by faith and not by philosophy (<em>Summa Theologiae</em>, I, q. 46, a. 2; <em>On the Eternity of the World</em>, around 1270). That is why he builds the ways so that they work <strong>even in an eternal world</strong>, as Maimonides did. In this he parts company with the <em>kalam</em>.</p>\n<h3>Two kinds of chain</h3>\n<p>So why does Thomas deny that the chain of causes can be infinite? Because he distinguishes two kinds of series (<em>Summa Theologiae</em>, I, q. 46, a. 2, ad 7):</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>‘Accidental’ series</th><th>‘Per se’ series</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Example</td><td>Grandfather, father, son, grandson</td><td>The hand moves the stick, the stick moves the stone</td></tr><tr><td>Do the causes act at the same time?</td><td>No: the grandfather may have died and the grandson be alive</td><td>Yes: if the hand stops, the stone stops</td></tr><tr><td>Can it be infinite?</td><td>Thomas admits that reason cannot rule it out</td><td>No: without a first, nothing would be moving now</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The ways are about the second kind of series. They are not looking for a first instant, but for something on which everything that exists and changes depends <strong>now</strong>.</p>\n<h3>They do not yet reach the God of the Bible</h3>\n<p>Each way ends in a first principle: a first mover, a first cause, a necessary being, a maximum, an intelligence. That this principle is one, simple, good, eternal, intelligent and free, Thomas argues in the following questions of the <em>Summa</em> (from 3 to 26). And that it is one God in three persons cannot be proved: it is a truth of faith (see topic <a href=\"#teoria/hf-fe-razon\">12</a>).</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: would a train of a thousand carriages without an engine move? And one with infinitely many carriages? Does it change anything that there are infinitely many?</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘the first way is the Big Bang’ or ‘modern physics has refuted the ways because it no longer needs a first mover’. The ways do not talk about a first instant, but about dependence. The discussion with modern physics does exist (inertia, which needs no mover to push; quantum chance), but it is a different discussion, and it is still going on.</p>\n\n<h2>7. The fifth way and the argument from design: they are not the same</h2>\n<p>The fifth way has very old antecedents. In <em>On the Nature of the Gods</em> (45 BC, Book II), Cicero puts this reasoning into the mouth of a Stoic: if you see a sundial or the mechanical sphere of the astronomer Posidonius, which reproduces the motion of the Sun, the Moon and the planets, you do not think they made themselves; how could you think so of the universe, which is far more perfect? In the 8th century, <strong>John of Damascus</strong> used it in his <em>Exposition of the Faith</em> (I, 3), a book Thomas knew well.</p>\n<p>But Thomas does not argue from <strong>complexity</strong>, but from <strong>direction</strong>. He notes that things without knowledge act ‘always or nearly always’ in the same way and towards what is best: the seed towards the plant, the stone downwards. And he compares it with an arrow: the arrow does not know where it is going, but it reaches the target because the archer directs it.</p>\n<p>Five centuries later, the argument changed. <strong>William Paley</strong>, in his <em>Natural Theology</em> (1802), imagined finding a watch in a field: its parts are so well fitted to measure the time that nobody would think they came together by chance. And the eye is more complex than a watch. Two criticisms changed the picture:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Hume</strong>, in the <em>Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion</em> (1779, before Paley), objected that the analogy between the world and a machine is weak. And even if it were accepted, why one designer and not several? Why not a clumsy one, or one who has since died? (see <a href=\"#teoria/hf-racionalismo\">‘Rationalism and empiricism’</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Darwin</strong>, in <em>On the Origin of Species</em> (1859), showed that natural selection explains the adaptation of living beings without the need for a designer. Darwin had read Paley as a student and admired him.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: which is harder to explain without an intelligence: that the eye is complex or that the laws of physics always hold? Does Darwin answer both questions or only one?</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘Darwin refuted the fifth way’. Darwin answers Paley, who talks about the complexity of living beings. The fifth way talks about the order and regularity of the whole of nature. Whether or not Darwin affects it is still debated.</p>\n\n<h2>8. After Thomas: the great criticisms and some defences</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Duns Scotus</strong> (around 1266-1308) rebuilt the proof starting from the possible: if it is possible for something to be produced, a first producer is possible, and a possible first producer must exist. <strong>William of Ockham</strong> (around 1287-1347) doubted whether it could be proved that there is no infinite series of causes that produce. He preferred another proof: the proof from what <strong>conserves</strong> things in existence now, because that cannot be an infinite series of present causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Leibniz</strong> defended the argument from contingency with the <strong>principle of sufficient reason</strong>: nothing exists without a reason why it is so and not otherwise. And he raised the most radical question: ‘why is there something rather than nothing?’ (<em>Principles of Nature and Grace</em>, § 7, 1714).</li>\n<li><strong>Hume</strong> attacked the foundation: the idea of cause arises from the habit of seeing one thing followed by another (<em>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</em>, 1748). With it we cannot leap beyond all possible experience. In the <em>Dialogues</em> (Part IX), one of the characters adds: if each part of a series is explained, what more is there to explain about the whole?</li>\n<li><strong>Kant</strong>, in the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (1781), in the part entitled ‘The Ideal of Pure Reason’, examined the three classic proofs: the ontological, the cosmological and the argument from design. His thesis has three steps:\n<ul><li>the ontological proof fails because ‘being is not a real predicate’: saying that something exists adds no property to it; a hundred thalers (silver coins of the time) that are thought of have the same properties as a hundred real thalers;</li><li>the cosmological proof, the one from contingency, in the end needs the ontological proof in order to identify the necessary being with the perfect being;</li><li>and causality is valid only within experience.</li></ul>\nBut Kant does <strong>not</strong> conclude that God does not exist: he concludes that theoretical reason can prove neither that he exists nor that he does not. God remains an idea of reason and a postulate of practical reason (see topic <a href=\"#teoria/hf-kant\">19</a>).</li>\n<li>On <strong>28 January 1948</strong>, the BBC broadcast a debate between the philosopher <strong>Bertrand Russell</strong>, who in the debate declared himself an agnostic, and the Jesuit and historian of philosophy <strong>Frederick Copleston</strong>. Copleston defended the argument from contingency, close to the third way. Russell replied that the question about the cause of the universe as a whole has no meaning: ‘the universe is just there, and that’s all’. Copleston replied that this was giving up on explaining. It is a good model of discussion: each listens to the other and answers the other’s reasons.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Against which way or proof</th><th>Objection</th><th>Possible reply (also disputed)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ockham</td><td>1st and 2nd</td><td>It cannot be proved that a series of causes is not infinite</td><td>That holds for the ‘accidental’ series; Thomas is talking about the ‘per se’ series</td></tr><tr><td>Hume</td><td>2nd and 3rd</td><td>Causality is not valid beyond experience; if each part is explained, so is the whole</td><td>Explaining each carriage does not explain why the train moves</td></tr><tr><td>Kant</td><td>Ontological and 3rd</td><td>Being is not a predicate; the cosmological proof hides the ontological one</td><td>Thomists reply that the third way does not start from a concept, but from what exists</td></tr><tr><td>Russell</td><td>3rd</td><td>The universe is a brute fact with no further explanation: it needs no cause</td><td>Copleston: saying that is refusing to ask the question</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: who do you think won the debate between Russell and Copleston, if anyone did? Can a question be meaningless even though all its words are understood?</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘Kant proved that God does not exist’. Kant holds that theoretical reason can prove neither that he exists nor that he does not. He himself believed in God for moral reasons.</p>\n\n<h2>9. What people say and what actually happened</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>What people say</th><th>What actually happened</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>‘Thomas invented the proofs of God’s existence’</td><td>He gathered and ordered arguments from Plato, Aristotle, Augustine, Anselm, Avicenna and Maimonides, among others, and he cited them.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>‘In the Middle Ages nobody doubted God’</td><td>Thomas writes out the atheist’s objections in their best form, and he wrote in order to argue with Muslims and pagans, with whom he shared no Scripture.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>‘The ways start from the Bible’</td><td>They start from experience. The Bible appears only in the <em>sed contra</em> (‘on the contrary’): ‘I am who I am’ (Exodus 3:14).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>‘Thomas accepted Anselm’s argument’</td><td>He expressly rejected it (I, q. 2, a. 1, ad 2). He did accept his path of degrees.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>‘The ways assume that the universe had a beginning’</td><td>Thomas held that this cannot be proved by reason, and he built the ways so that they also work if the world is eternal.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>‘Aristotle’s God and Thomas’s God are the same’</td><td>The unmoved mover neither creates nor knows the world. Thomas uses the argument, but changes the God it arrives at.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>‘The five ways are five independent and complete proofs’</td><td>The first three share a structure; and all of them need the following questions to reach God’s attributes.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<h2>10. Chronogram</h2>\n<p>Each argument has its own history, and Thomas stands at the point where five of them cross. To see how each argument passes from one thinker to another, open the map <a href=\"#genealogias/plano/dios\">‘Proofs of the existence of God’</a> in Genealogies: each argument is an underground line, Thomas is the station where the five ways cross, and the dashed red lines mark the clashes between authors. To see who overlapped with whom in time, there is the chronogram <a href=\"#cronogramas/B-MED-VIAS-01\">‘The five ways and their antecedents’</a>. Below, the same story in a table.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Author and work</th><th>What it contributes</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Around 350 BC</td><td>Plato, <em>Laws</em> X</td><td>The soul, origin of all motion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4th century BC</td><td>Aristotle, <em>Physics</em> VIII and <em>Metaphysics</em> XII</td><td>The unmoved mover</td></tr>\n<tr><td>45 BC</td><td>Cicero, <em>On the Nature of the Gods</em></td><td>The order of the world, like a sundial</td></tr>\n<tr><td>388-395</td><td>Augustine, <em>On Free Choice of the Will</em></td><td>From truth to God</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Around 524</td><td>Boethius, <em>The Consolation of Philosophy</em></td><td>From the imperfect good to the perfect</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8th century</td><td>John of Damascus, <em>Exposition of the Faith</em></td><td>The order of the world</td></tr>\n<tr><td>9th century</td><td>Al-Kindi</td><td>The world had a beginning, so it has a cause (the argument the <em>kalam</em> will make its own)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Around 1020-1027</td><td>Avicenna, <em>The Healing</em></td><td>The possible and the necessary in itself</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1076 and 1077-1078</td><td>Anselm, <em>Monologion</em> and <em>Proslogion</em></td><td>The degrees; the ontological argument</td></tr>\n<tr><td>12th century</td><td>Averroes</td><td>Return to the proof from motion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Around 1190</td><td>Maimonides, <em>Guide for the Perplexed</em></td><td>Proving God even in an eternal world</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Around 1259-1265</td><td>Thomas, <em>Summa contra Gentiles</em></td><td>The proof from motion, at length</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Around 1266-1268</td><td>Thomas, <em>Summa Theologiae</em>, I, q. 2</td><td>The five ways</td></tr>\n<tr><td>14th century</td><td>Duns Scotus, Ockham</td><td>Proofs from the possible and from conservation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1641</td><td>Descartes, <em>Meditations</em></td><td>The ontological argument returns</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1714</td><td>Leibniz, <em>Principles of Nature and Grace</em></td><td>The principle of sufficient reason</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1779</td><td>Hume, <em>Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion</em></td><td>Criticism of design and of causality</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1781</td><td>Kant, <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em></td><td>Criticism of the three proofs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1802</td><td>Paley, <em>Natural Theology</em></td><td>The watch and the eye</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1859</td><td>Darwin, <em>On the Origin of Species</em></td><td>Adaptation without a designer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1948</td><td>Russell and Copleston, on the BBC</td><td>Contingency, up for debate</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<h2>11. Commented texts</h2>\n<p>Texts 2, 6 and 7 are given in full, in our own translation from the Latin. The others, written in Greek, Latin, Arabic or German, are given as faithful summaries, also in our own translation. Maimonides wrote in Arabic, though in Hebrew letters.</p>\n\n<h3>Text 1. The mover that moves as the beloved does (Aristotle, <em>Metaphysics</em> XII 7, 1072a-b; faithful summary)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>There is something that moves without being moved, and that is eternal, substance and actuality. It moves as the desirable and the intelligible do: it moves without moving. It moves, then, as that which is loved, and the other things move because they are moved. On such a principle the heavens depend, and nature depends.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Aristotle does not imagine a God who pushes the world, but one who attracts it. Everything that changes tends towards the perfection of that mover, which does nothing but think. That is why the unmoved mover is at once the cause of motion and the end of everything. Thomas will accept that God also moves as an end, but not the idea of a God who does not know the world.</p>\n\n<h3>Text 2. That than which nothing greater can be thought (Anselm, <em>Proslogion</em>, ch. 2; our own full translation)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>So then, Lord, you who give understanding to faith, grant me to understand, as far as you know it to be good for me, that you exist as we believe and that you are what we believe. And we believe that you are something than which nothing greater can be thought. Or is there no such nature, since ‘the fool has said in his heart: there is no God’ [Psalm 14:1; in the Vulgate, 13:1]? But surely this same fool, when he hears what I am saying, ‘something than which nothing greater can be thought’, understands what he hears; and what he understands is in his understanding, even if he does not understand that it exists. For it is one thing for something to be in the understanding and another to understand that it exists. When a painter thinks out beforehand what he is going to make, he has it in his understanding, but he does not yet understand that it exists, because he has not yet made it. When he has painted it, he has it in his understanding and also understands that it exists, because he has now made it. So even the fool must admit that something than which nothing greater can be thought is at least in the understanding, because when he hears it he understands it, and whatever is understood is in the understanding. And surely that than which nothing greater can be thought cannot be in the understanding alone. For if it is in the understanding alone, it can be thought to exist in reality as well, which is greater. If, then, that than which nothing greater can be thought is in the understanding alone, that very thing than which nothing greater can be thought is something than which something greater can be thought. But surely this cannot be. Therefore something than which nothing greater can be thought exists beyond any doubt, both in the understanding and in reality.</p></blockquote>\n<p>The text is a prayer: Anselm is not arguing with anyone, he is speaking to God. It is what Anselm called ‘faith seeking understanding’, which was the book’s first title. The example of the painter separates two things we tend to confuse: having something in our head and knowing that it exists. The whole argument depends on one step: that existing in reality makes something ‘greater’ than existing only in thought.</p>\n\n<h3>Text 3. The lost island (Gaunilo, <em>On Behalf of the Fool</em>, 6; faithful summary)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>They say that somewhere in the ocean there is an island, which they call ‘the lost island’ because it is difficult or impossible to find, richer in every kind of good thing than any inhabited land. If someone tells me this, I understand it without difficulty. But if he adds that this island must exist, because, if it did not exist, any real land would be more excellent than it, then either I shall think he is joking, or I shall not know which of us is the greater fool: I, for believing him, or he, for believing he has proved something.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Gaunilo does not deny that God exists: he was a monk. He denies that the argument is a good one. His method is to apply the same reasoning to something else and see that it leads to an absurdity. It is the method of the counterexample, the same one used in logic to prove that an argument is not valid.</p>\n\n<h3>Text 4. The necessary and the possible (Avicenna, <em>Metaphysics</em> of <em>The Healing</em>, I, 6; faithful summary)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Everything that exists, if it is considered in itself, is either necessary or possible. The necessary in itself is that which, if it is supposed not to exist, results in something impossible. The possible in itself is that which, whether it is supposed to exist or not to exist, results in nothing impossible. The possible, considered in itself, has no more reason to exist than not to exist. That is why, if it exists, it is necessary through another, which makes it exist.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Avicenna does not ask whether the world began, but why something exists that might not exist. Even if the series of possible things were eternal, each of them would still need another to make it exist, and so would the whole. From here comes the third way, and the distinction between what something is and the fact that it exists.</p>\n\n<h3>Text 5. Not everything can come into being and pass away (Maimonides, <em>Guide for the Perplexed</em>, II, 1, third speculation; faithful summary)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>We see things that exist. If all of them could come into being and pass away, at some moment all of them would have ceased to exist, and then nothing would exist, because nothing would be left to make them exist again. But we see that they exist. Therefore there must be something that cannot come into being or pass away, that exists necessarily in itself and not through a cause.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Compare it with the third way (text 6): the structure is almost the same. Maimonides wrote this proof in Arabic in Egypt around 1190, and Thomas read it in Latin a few decades later.</p>\n\n<h3>Text 6. The five ways (Thomas Aquinas, <em>Summa Theologiae</em>, I, q. 2, a. 3; our own full translation)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p><strong>Article 3. Whether God exists.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Objection 1.</strong> It seems that God does not exist. If one of two contraries were infinite, it would completely destroy the other. And by the word ‘God’ is understood precisely an infinite good. Therefore, if God existed, there would be no evil at all. But there is evil in the world. Therefore God does not exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Objection 2.</strong> Further, what can be done with fewer principles is not done with more. And it seems that everything we see in the world can be explained by other principles, even supposing that God does not exist: natural things are reduced to one principle, which is nature; and what is done on purpose is reduced to another, which is human reason or will. Therefore there is no need to suppose that God exists.</p>\n<p><strong>On the contrary,</strong> in Exodus (3:14) it is said, in the mouth of God: ‘I am who I am’.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer.</strong> It must be said that the existence of God can be proved in five ways.</p>\n<p><strong>The first and clearest</strong> is the one that starts from motion. It is certain, and evident to the senses, that in this world some things are moved. Now, whatever is moved is moved by another. For nothing is moved except in so far as it is in potentiality with respect to that towards which it is moved; and something moves in so far as it is in actuality. To move is nothing other than to bring something from potentiality to actuality, and nothing can pass from potentiality to actuality except through something that is in actuality. Thus what is actually hot, like fire, makes wood, which is potentially hot, actually hot, and in this way moves and alters it. But it is not possible for one and the same thing to be at once in actuality and in potentiality in the same respect, but only in different respects: what is actually hot cannot at the same time be potentially hot, but it is at the same time potentially cold. It is impossible, therefore, for something to be, in the same respect and in the same way, both mover and moved, or to move itself. Therefore whatever is moved must be moved by another. If that which moves it is also moved, it will in turn have to be moved by another, and that by another. But here one cannot go on to infinity, because then there would be no first mover and, consequently, no other mover either: second movers do not move unless they are moved by the first, just as the stick does not move unless the hand moves it. Therefore it is necessary to arrive at a first mover that is moved by nothing. And this is what everyone understands by God.</p>\n<p><strong>The second way</strong> starts from the notion of efficient cause. In sensible things we find an order of efficient causes. But it is not found, nor is it possible, that something should be the efficient cause of itself, because then it would be prior to itself, which is impossible. Nor is it possible to go on to infinity in efficient causes. For in all ordered efficient causes, the first is the cause of the intermediate and the intermediate is the cause of the last, whether the intermediate ones are many or only one. And if the cause is taken away, the effect is taken away. Therefore, if there were no first among efficient causes, there would be neither a last nor an intermediate one. But if one went on to infinity in efficient causes, there would be no first efficient cause, and so there would be neither a last effect nor intermediate efficient causes, which is plainly false. Therefore it is necessary to admit a first efficient cause, to which everyone gives the name of God.</p>\n<p><strong>The third way</strong> starts from the possible and the necessary, and runs as follows. We find among things some that can be and not be, for we see that some come into being and pass away, and therefore they can be and not be. But it is impossible for everything that exists to be like this, because what can not be, at some time is not. If, then, all things can not be, there was a time when there was nothing. And if this were true, there would be nothing even now, because what is not begins to be only through something that already is. If there was no being at all, it was impossible for anything to begin to be, and so there would now be nothing, which is plainly false. Therefore not all beings are merely possible: there must be among things something necessary. Now, every necessary thing either has the cause of its necessity in another, or it does not. And it is not possible to go on to infinity in necessary things that have a cause of their necessity, just as it is not in efficient causes, as has been proved. Therefore it is necessary to admit something that is necessary in itself, which does not have the cause of its necessity in another, but is the cause of the necessity of the others. And this everyone calls God.</p>\n<p><strong>The fourth way</strong> starts from the degrees that are found in things. In things there is something more and less good, true and noble, and so with other similar qualities. But ‘more’ and ‘less’ are said of different things according as they approach in different ways something that is in the highest degree; thus, that is hotter which comes closer to what is hottest. There is, therefore, something that is truest, best and noblest, and consequently being in the highest degree, for what is most true is most fully being, as is said in Book II of the <em>Metaphysics</em> [of Aristotle]. And what is said to be greatest in a genus is the cause of everything that belongs to that genus, as fire, which is hottest, is the cause of everything hot, as is said in the same book. Therefore there is something that is, for all beings, the cause of their being, of their goodness and of every perfection. And this we call God.</p>\n<p><strong>The fifth way</strong> starts from the governance of things. We see that some things that lack knowledge, that is, natural bodies, act for an end. This can be seen because they always, or nearly always, act in the same way, so as to achieve what is best; from which it is clear that they reach their end not by chance, but by intention. But what has no knowledge does not tend to an end unless it is directed by someone who knows and understands, as the arrow is directed by the archer. Therefore there is something intelligent by which all natural things are ordered to their end. And this we call God.</p>\n<p><strong>Reply to objection 1.</strong> As Augustine says in the <em>Enchiridion</em> [ch. 11], ‘God, being supremely good, would in no way allow there to be any evil in his works if he were not so omnipotent and so good as to bring good even out of evil’. It belongs, then, to the infinite goodness of God to allow evils to exist in order to draw goods from them.</p>\n<p><strong>Reply to objection 2.</strong> Since nature acts for a determined end under the direction of a higher agent, what nature does must also be traced back to God as to its first cause. In the same way, what is done on purpose must also be traced back to a cause higher than human reason and will, because these are changeable and can fail. And everything that is changeable and can fail must be traced back to a first principle that is unmoved and necessary in itself, as has been shown.</p></blockquote>\n<p>The article follows the order of the whole <em>Summa</em>: objections, an authority on the opposite side, the master’s answer and the reply to each objection. Notice three things. The five ways begin with something that is ‘evident to the senses’. Each one rests on a principle that Thomas considers self-evident (nothing passes from potentiality to actuality by itself, nothing is the cause of itself, nothing comes from nothing). And the replies to the objections do not deny evil or nature: they build them into the reasoning.</p>\n<p>The reply to objection 2 rests on a medieval distinction: the <strong>first cause</strong> and the <strong>secondary causes</strong>. Natural causes, such as nature or the human will, really do explain what happens in the world, and Thomas does not deny it. But, according to him, they do not explain themselves: they need a first cause that keeps them in being. These are two different levels, and neither cancels out the other. That is why, for Thomas, explaining something by natural causes does not compete with explaining it by God. Whether this really answers the naturalist is still being debated.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: the reply to objection 1 says that God allows evil in order to draw goods from it. Does that seem to you a sufficient answer to the problem of evil? What would someone who is suffering say to it?</p>\n\n<h3>Text 7. Why Thomas does not accept Anselm’s argument (<em>Summa Theologiae</em>, I, q. 2, a. 1, ad 2; our own full translation)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>It may be that someone who hears the word ‘God’ does not understand that it means something than which nothing greater can be thought, for some have believed that God was a body. But even supposing that everyone understood that the word ‘God’ signifies what is said, that is, that than which nothing greater can be thought, it does not follow from this that they understand that what the word signifies exists in reality, but only in the apprehension of the understanding [that is, only in thought]. And it cannot be argued that it exists in reality unless it is granted that in reality there is something than which nothing greater can be thought; and this is not granted by those who hold that God does not exist.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Thomas does not name Anselm, but he sums up his argument in the objection. Deep down, Thomas reproaches him for taking for granted what he wants to prove: what in logic is called <strong>begging the question</strong> (see the Logic Corner). Whether the reproach is fair is still debated.</p>\n\n<h3>Text 8. Being is not a real predicate (Kant, <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>, A598-599/B626-627; faithful summary)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>‘Being’ is obviously not a real predicate, that is, a concept of something that could be added to the concept of a thing. It is merely the positing of a thing. A hundred real thalers do not contain anything more than a hundred possible thalers. That is why, when I say ‘God exists’, I add no new predicate to the concept of God: I only posit the object with all its predicates.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Kant attacks step 3 of Anselm’s argument: that existing makes something ‘greater’. For him, existence is not one more property, like colour or size. That something exists can be known only through experience, and we do not find God in experience. The thaler was a silver coin of his time.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: would a unicorn that is thought of and a unicorn that existed have different properties? Are you convinced by Kant, or do you think that existing really is ‘more’ than not existing?</p>\n\n<h2>12. Dictionary</h2>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Actuality and potentiality</td><td>A pair of concepts from Aristotle. Potentiality is the capacity to become something; actuality, already being it. Cold wood is potentially hot; fire, actually hot.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unmoved mover</td><td>In Aristotle, the principle that moves everything without moving, because it is pure actuality. It moves as an end, ‘as the beloved does’.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Efficient cause</td><td>That which produces something: the sculptor is the efficient cause of the statue.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Infinite regress</td><td>A chain of explanations or causes that never ends. The first three ways hold that, in certain chains, it is impossible.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>‘Per se’ and ‘accidental’ series</td><td>In a per se series, each cause acts now thanks to the one before (hand, stick, stone). In an accidental series, it does not (grandfather, father, son). Thomas denies only that the first kind can be infinite.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Being (entity)</td><td>That which is, that which exists.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Contingent</td><td>What exists but might not exist.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Necessary</td><td>What cannot not exist. For Avicenna, it can be so ‘in itself’ or ‘through another’.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Essence and existence</td><td>What something is and the fact that it exists. In creatures they are distinct; in God, according to Avicenna and Thomas, they are the same.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Final cause</td><td>That for the sake of which something is done or towards which it tends: the plant is the final cause of the seed. In Aristotle it applies to the whole of nature, not only to living beings.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>First cause and secondary causes</td><td>The secondary causes are those of the world: nature, the human will. The first cause is the one that, according to Thomas, keeps them in being. The one does not cancel out the others.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Transcendentals</td><td>Perfections that everything that exists has by the fact of existing: being, goodness, truth (and, for some, beauty). They carry no limit with them.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Demonstration <em>quia</em> and <em>propter quid</em></td><td>The first goes from effect to cause; the second, from cause to effect. The ways are <em>quia</em> demonstrations.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><em>A priori</em> / <em>a posteriori</em></td><td>Today, in Kant’s sense: an <em>a priori</em> argument starts only from concepts (Anselm’s); an <em>a posteriori</em> one, from experience (the five ways). In the Middle Ages, ‘<em>a priori</em>’ meant ‘from cause to effect’.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ontological argument</td><td>A proof of God’s existence from his concept. The name was given to it by Kant.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cosmological argument</td><td>A proof that starts from the fact that a world exists: motion, causes, contingency. The first three ways are of this kind.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Teleological argument or argument from design</td><td>A proof that starts from the order or purposiveness of nature. The fifth way is usually classified here, but it argues from direction towards an end and not from complexity (see section 7).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><em>Kalam</em></td><td>The rational theology of Islam. Its best-known argument starts from the claim that the world had a beginning.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Principle of sufficient reason</td><td>Leibniz’s principle: nothing exists or happens without a reason why it is so and not otherwise.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><em>Sed contra</em></td><td>‘On the contrary’: the part of the scholastic article in which an authority opposed to the objections is cited.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>"}});
